# Cursor Rules for React TypeScript Project

# File Organization
- Keep all React components in the `src/components` directory
- Place page components in the `src/pages` directory
- Store custom hooks in the `src/hooks` directory
- Keep utility functions in the `src/utils` directory
- Place theme-related code in the `src/theme` directory
- Store static assets in the `src/assets` directory

# Component Structure
- Each component should have its own directory under `src/components`
- Component files should be named using PascalCase (e.g., `Button.tsx`)
- Include a `index.ts` file in each component directory for clean exports
- Keep component-specific styles in the same directory as the component

# TypeScript Guidelines
- Use TypeScript for all new files
- Define interfaces and types in separate files under `src/types` if they're shared
- Use `.tsx` extension for files containing JSX
- Use `.ts` extension for plain TypeScript files

# Testing
- Place test files next to the components they test
- Use `.test.ts` or `.test.tsx` for test files
- Follow the pattern: `ComponentName.test.tsx`

# Styling
- Use CSS modules for component-specific styles
- Keep global styles in `src/index.css`
- Follow BEM naming convention for CSS classes
- Use theme variables from `src/theme` for consistent styling

# Code Quality
- Use functional components with hooks
- Keep components small and focused
- Use proper TypeScript types and avoid `any`
- Follow React best practices and patterns

# Git
- Keep commits atomic and focused
- Use meaningful commit messages
- Follow conventional commits format

# Dependencies
- Keep dependencies up to date
- Document major dependency changes in PRs
- Use exact versions in package.json

# Performance
- Implement proper code splitting
- Use React.memo() for expensive components
- Optimize images and assets
- Follow React performance best practices 